ect were collected\nfrom online data sources. Traffic data was obtained from the Victorian\nDepartment of Transport traffic signal volume datasets, while weather\ndata was collected from the Australian Bureau of Meteorology (BoM)\nwebsite using automated data extraction techniques.\n\nThe extracted data was then cleaned, transformed, and saved locally as\nCSV files throughout different stages of the workflow. Saving\nintermediate CSV files allowed the preprocessing, feature engineering,\nand modelling stages to remain organised and reproducible without\nrepeatedly downloading or reprocessing the raw data.\n\nThe datasets were stored within structured project directories,\nincluding:\n\n-   data/raw/traffic\n-   data/raw/weather\n-   data/raw/events\n-   data/processed/cleaned\n-   data/processed/merged\n-   data/processed/final\n\nThese folders were used to organise the raw downloaded datasets, cleaned\ndatasets, merged datasets, and final feature-engineered datasets used\nthroughout the traffic congestion prediction pipeline.\n\nTraffic Data Cleaning and Preprocessing\n\nRaw traffic datasets often contain inconsistent formats, duplicate\nrecords, unnecessary columns, and missing values. In this stage, the\ntraffic data is cleaned and transformed into a structured format\nsuitable for analysis and machine learning.\n\nKey preprocessing tasks include:\n\n-   Standardising column formats\n-   Removing invalid or incomplete entries\n-   Converting date and time fields into usable datetime formats\n-   Organising traffic counts by location and time interval\n\nThis process improves data reliability and ensures accurate downstream\nanalysis.\n\nDataset Overview\n\nBefore beginning the modelling process, it is important to first\nunderstand the structure and quality of the dataset being used. In this\nsection, the integrated traffic and weather dataset is explored to\nidentify the available features, data types, missing values, and overall\ndataset behaviour.\n\nUnderstan